== fuller page ==
In C4, a word is a type when the system knows how to effect it: "give it
real behavior, recognise it, use it." It does not need a row in the table
[vtype]. Corwin's words: "a virtual type simply means a word that we honor
as it was a word we know how to effect as a type within our taxonomy even
though it does not appear as a taxonomy" [vpress].

His chosen example is LIST. The L line of m/t reads LAYOUT, LIBRARY, LINK,
LICENSE. It has no LIST, and none is due [lrow]. Yet the shop makes lists.
A small file, l/l, pins each of eight brackets, ( ) [ ] { } < >, to its own
hash, and that registry is what makes a list a list, with no table word
anywhere in the loop [ll]. The file is on disk, and it is still marked
CANDIDATE: the ruling settled the principle, not that one file [cand].

This undoes a wrong question. "Is LIST in the table?" was checking the
wrong ledger. The rule "decouples 'is this real?' from 'is this in the
table?'" A type is real when something can do it [ledger].

C4-LANG lists six such words so far, each a word and a promise: TYPE (or
kind), theme, skin, QUERY, word and hunk [six]. One of them, word, holds the
whole grammar together; it comes back on the keeping page.

