== narration ==
# Take A, "the company talks to you": every voice speaks to the listener.
[picture A]
narrator: Here's the L door. Can you count the four names on it?
spoke: Layout, library, link, license. No list.
narrator: List is not one of them.
pin: But look what I've got. I can make a list, see? Brackets and all.
narrator: We can do it. So list is a real word, too.
spoke: A word we can do is a real word. Remember that one for me.
[fuller A]
narrator [vtype]: In {C4|C four}, a word is a type when the system knows how to do it. It doesn't need a row in the table.
pin [vpress]: Corwin's own words: a virtual type is a word we honour as a word we know how to effect, even though it isn't in the taxonomy.
spoke [lrow]: Look at the L line with me: layout, library, link, license. No list, and none is due.
pin [ll]: But there's a little file, {l/l|ell ell}. It pins eight brackets, each one to its own hash. That's what makes a list a list.
spoke [cand]: It's still marked candidate. The ruling settled the idea, not that one file.
narrator [ledger]: So asking whether list is in the table was checking the wrong ledger. A type is real when something can do it.
pin [six]: The language book counts six words like this so far. One of them, word, comes back soon. Watch for it with me.

# Take B, "the lap reader": one close voice reads to you; Spoke and Pin
# mostly talk to each other, and turn to you only in a short aside.
[picture B]
narrator: The L door has four names. List is not one of them.
spoke: Pin, there's no list on the L door.
pin: There's a list in my paws, though.
narrator: But Pin can make a list. We can do it.
spoke: Then list is a real word.
pin: That's what I wrote.
[fuller B]
narrator [vtype]: In {C4|C four}, a word is a type when the system knows how to effect it: give it real behaviour, recognise it, use it. It does not need a row in the table.
narrator [vpress]: Corwin put it this way: a virtual type simply means a word that we honor as a word we know how to effect, even though it does not appear in the taxonomy.
spoke [lrow]: The L line says layout, library, link, license.
pin [lrow]: No list. None due.
narrator [ll]: Yet the shop makes lists. A small file, {l/l|ell ell}, pins each of eight brackets to its own hash, with no table word anywhere in the loop.
pin [cand]: I wrote it down as candidate. The principle landed, not the file.
narrator [ledger]: Is list in the table? That was the wrong ledger. A type is real when something can do it.
spoke [six]: Six words like that so far, Pin.
pin [six]: Six. Word is one of them. Hold on to that one.

